  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/613eaf413822013885a22df61a4bf32f7cd763bf11db71ab7b8769e19c0369b0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n- It is continuous at $x=3.$\n- It has removable discontinuities at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$",
  "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\\ne f(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, I and III are true while II is false.",
